WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.036 --> 00:00:17.527
Early the next morning Ned and Conseil joined me on the platform.

2
00:00:17.528 --> 00:00:23.949
"Well, Sir Naturalist," said Ned, addressing me in a cheerful tone, "where are we today?"

3
00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:28.203
"We're floating on the surface of the Mediterranean Sea," I replied.

4
00:00:28.204 --> 00:00:33.703
"I don't believe that for one minute," said Ned. "We couldn't have crossed the Isthmus of Suez."

5
00:00:33.704 --> 00:00:38.703
"If Master says it's true, then it must be true," said loyal Conseil.

6
00:00:38.704 --> 00:00:45.513
"Thank you, Conseil." I described for them how the Nautilus had swiftly passed through Captain Nemo's underwater tunnel.

7
00:00:45.514 --> 00:00:53.838
"That shoreline over there is Egypt. You have such good eyes, Ned, that you can probably see the Port Said jetty stretching into the sea."

8
00:00:53.839 --> 00:00:56.751
Ned looked in the direction I pointed.

9
00:00:56.752 --> 00:01:03.600
"Why, I believe you're correct, Professor. We are in the Mediterranean. Good!

10
00:01:03.601 --> 00:01:07.663
Now let's discuss our own business where no one will overhear us."

11
00:01:07.664 --> 00:01:12.648
Ned led the way to a small space behind the electric light on top of the submarine.

12
00:01:12.649 --> 00:01:16.330
"What I have to say is very simple," Ned began.

13
00:01:16.331 --> 00:01:23.822
"We're in Europe now. Before Captain Nemo's whims drag us back to the Pacific Ocean, I want to leave the Nautilus."

14
00:01:23.823 --> 00:01:30.473
I didn't want to be responsible for restricting the freedom of my companions, but I really had no desire to leave.

15
00:01:30.474 --> 00:01:37.711
Thanks to Captain Nemo and his submarine, I was rewriting my book on deep-sea life while living in the very midst of it.

16
00:01:37.712 --> 00:01:41.647
I didn't want to abandon the Nautilus before my research was finished.

17
00:01:41.648 --> 00:01:46.473
"Ned, do you regret the time you've spent underwater?" I asked.

18
00:01:46.474 --> 00:01:49.711
The harpooner paused for a moment before replying.

19
00:01:49.712 --> 00:01:55.870
Then he crossed his arms and said, "I don't regret it, but I want this voyage to end."

20
00:01:55.871 --> 00:02:01.362
"I have to admit I don't know when that will be," I said. "But it will end."

21
00:02:01.363 --> 00:02:09.529
"I agree with Master," said Conseil. "And it's possible that Captain Nemo will let us go after he has crossed every sea on earth."

22
00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:17.267
"I don't think that will happen." I shook my head. "We know too much about the captain and his submarine for him to let us go.

23
00:02:17.268 --> 00:02:22.013
But in six months, perhaps we'll find another opportunity to escape."

24
00:02:22.014 --> 00:02:28.172
"Six months!" exclaimed Ned. "Where on earth will we be in six months?"

25
00:02:28.173 --> 00:02:36.259
"I don't know," I replied. "But whenever we decide to escape, we must succeed on our first attempt.

26
00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:41.926
If we fail, we'll never have another opportunity, and Captain Nemo will never forgive us."

27
00:02:41.927 --> 00:02:45.751
Ned was still determined to escape as soon as possible.

28
00:02:45.752 --> 00:02:51.823
He outlined a plan that involved taking the small boat one night when we were along the European coastline.

29
00:02:51.824 --> 00:02:55.482
"Do you want to know what I think of your plan?" I asked.

30
00:02:55.483 --> 00:02:58.831
"Certainly, Professor Aronnax," said Ned.

31
00:02:58.832 --> 00:03:06.990
"I think we'll never have the chance to escape because Captain Nemo will always be on guard, especially when we're near Europe." I stood up.

32
00:03:06.991 --> 00:03:09.531
"And now not another word on the subject.

33
00:03:09.532 --> 00:03:14.902
The day that you're ready to escape, let us know and we'll follow you. I leave everything up to you."

34
00:03:14.903 --> 00:03:18.378
The next day we reached the Greek islands.

35
00:03:18.379 --> 00:03:25.227
I wanted to spend some time studying the marine life, but the panels in the gallery remained closed until evening.

36
00:03:25.228 --> 00:03:28.862
After supper Captain Nemo joined me in the gallery.

37
00:03:28.863 --> 00:03:36.799
He seemed preoccupied and didn't speak to me, but kept peering through the windows. Suddenly a man appeared in the water.

38
00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:42.609
"Look, Captain!" I cried. "That poor man has been shipwrecked. We must save him!"

39
00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:45.958
The captain didn't answer me, but leaned against the window.

40
00:03:45.959 --> 00:03:51.204
To my astonishment, the man in the water approached and pressed his face to the glass.

41
00:03:51.205 --> 00:03:55.370
Captain Nemo gave him a signal and the man headed for the surface.

42
00:03:55.371 --> 00:03:58.616
"Don't worry about him," said Captain Nemo.

43
00:03:58.617 --> 00:04:05.695
"That's Nicholas, a bold diver who swims from one Greek island to the next. His nickname is 'The Fish.'"

44
00:04:05.696 --> 00:04:09.600
Captain Nemo went over to a chest in a corner of the room.

45
00:04:09.601 --> 00:04:15.838
I could barely contain my shock as he opened it, revealing a supply of gold bars!

46
00:04:15.839 --> 00:04:23.426
He seemed to have forgotten I was there as he carefully counted the bars. There must have been two tons of gold in that chest.

47
00:04:23.427 --> 00:04:27.601
Where did he get it all and what was he going to do with it? I wondered.

48
00:04:27.602 --> 00:04:31.418
The captain closed the chest and wrote an address on it in Greek.

49
00:04:31.419 --> 00:04:36.593
He pressed a button to communicate with the crew, and within a few minutes four men appeared.

50
00:04:36.594 --> 00:04:41.077
With great difficulty, they removed the heavy chest from the gallery.

51
00:04:41.078 --> 00:04:45.164
Captain Nemo turned to me. "What were you saying, Professor?"

52
00:04:45.165 --> 00:04:50.402
"I wasn't saying anything," I replied, still in shock at what I'd witnessed.

53
00:04:50.403 --> 00:04:52.656
"Then I will bid you good night."

54
00:04:52.657 --> 00:04:56.489
I returned to my cabin very puzzled about what I'd seen.

55
00:04:56.490 --> 00:05:04.481
I tried to fall asleep, but I kept wondering about the connection between the appearance of the diver and the removal of the chest filled with gold.

56
00:05:04.482 --> 00:05:09.720
Soon I felt the rolling movements that signaled the Nautilus had risen to the surface.

57
00:05:09.721 --> 00:05:15.299
I heard steps upon the platform—someone was unfastening the small boat and launching it!

58
00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:18.791
The boat struck the side of the submarine and then all was silent.

59
00:05:18.792 --> 00:05:27.109
Two hours later I heard the same noises and knew that the small boat had returned, and the Nautilus was sinking below the waves.

60
00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:32.617
The next day I told Ned and Conseil about the strange events of the previous evening.

61
00:05:32.618 --> 00:05:36.871
"How much do you think the gold is worth, Master?" asked Conseil.

62
00:05:36.872 --> 00:05:40.450
"Where was the gold taken?" asked Ned.

63
00:05:40.451 --> 00:05:45.776
I had no answer for either question, so I returned to working on my notes in the gallery.

64
00:05:45.777 --> 00:05:50.688
Around five o'clock I began to feel so hot that I took off my jacket.

65
00:05:50.689 --> 00:05:57.014
"Perhaps I'm getting a fever?" I thought. I continued my work, but I kept feeling hotter and hotter.

66
00:05:57.015 --> 00:06:00.451
"Could there be a fire onboard?" I asked myself.

67
00:06:00.452 --> 00:06:06.522
I was about to leave the gallery to check when Captain Nemo entered. He went over to the thermometer.

68
00:06:06.523 --> 00:06:09.665
"Forty-two degrees," he said.

69
00:06:09.666 --> 00:06:13.760
"If it gets much hotter, we won't be able to stand it," I said.

70
00:06:13.761 --> 00:06:20.236
"Oh, it won't get any hotter if we don't want it to. We can simply move away from the source that's producing this heat."

71
00:06:20.237 --> 00:06:23.649
"So it's outside?" I was still puzzled.

72
00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:28.403
"Yes, we're floating in a current of boiling water. Look!"

73
00:06:28.404 --> 00:06:32.736
He opened the panels to reveal a white and boiling sea.

74
00:06:32.737 --> 00:06:37.569
"I wanted you to see the unusual spectacle of an underwater volcano."

75
00:06:37.570 --> 00:06:42.315
I pressed my hand to a window, but the heat was so great that I sprang back.

76
00:06:42.316 --> 00:06:46.974
The Nautilus had stopped moving and the temperature in the room was unbearable.

77
00:06:46.975 --> 00:06:51.490
"We can't stay one moment longer in this boiling water!" I cried.

78
00:06:51.491 --> 00:06:55.371
"You're right," said Captain Nemo, as calm as ever.

79
00:06:55.372 --> 00:06:58.728
He gave an order and the submarine turned around.

80
00:06:58.729 --> 00:07:02.450
Fifteen minutes later we were breathing fresh air on the surface.

81
00:07:02.451 --> 00:07:11.853
That's when a startling thought occurred to me: if Ned had chosen this part of the Mediterranean for our escape, we never would have survived.
